/* book: lead: Passband modulation puts bits onto a sine-wave carrier so they can travel through air, cable or fibre. Binary amplitude shift keying (ASK) changes the carrier amplitude for the two bits; binary frequency shift keying (BFSK) keeps the amplitude and switches between two frequencies. Both are cheap, so they run car key fobs, RFID tags, 433 MHz remotes and low-rate telemetry. */
/* book Figure 6.1.1: The worked example’s bits 1 0 1 1 0 at 10 kb/s. OOK sends 3 cycles of a 30 kHz carrier for each 1 and nothing for each 0; BFSK sends 3 cycles (30 kHz) for a 1 and 2 cycles (20 kHz) for a 0, so its envelope never changes. */
