/* book: lead: Binary phase shift keying (BPSK) carries one bit in the phase of the carrier: cos(2πfc t) for a 1 and its negative for a 0. The two signals are exact opposites (antipodal), which puts them as far apart as possible for a given energy, so BPSK has the lowest bit error probability of any binary scheme in white Gaussian noise. GPS, deep-space links and the control channels of mobile systems use it whe */
/* book Figure 6.2.1: BPSK for the bits 1 0 1 1 0 (carrier drawn with 2 cycles per bit). The carrier flips by 180° whenever the bit changes. The correlator output ramps up to +√Eb during a 1 and down to −√Eb during a 0; the sample at each bit end is compared with 0. */
