/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - s(t) = ±√(2Eb/Tb) cos(2πfc t), 0 ≤ t < Tb
   - Eb is the bit energy in joules (1 Ω reference), Tb the bit time in s and fc the carrier in Hz; + sends bit 1 (phase 0) and − sends bit 0 (phase π).
   - The envelope is constant, so the power amplifier can run near saturation.
   - The receiver multiplies r(t) = s(t) + w(t), where w(t) is white noise of two-sided density N0/2, by the unit-energy basis function φ(t) = √(2/Tb)cos(2πfc t) and integrates over the bit:
   - The integral runs over 0 to Tb. y is in √J (volts × √seconds with a 1 Ω reference), not volts. n is Gaussian with mean 0 and variance N0/2.
   - The decision is the sign of y: y > 0 → 1, y < 0 → 0.
   - The local carrier must match the incoming phase.
   - A 1 is misread when the noise pulls y below zero, which needs n < −√Eb.
   - Pb = Q(√Eb/σ) = Q(√(2Eb/N0))
   - N0 is the one-sided noise density in W/Hz and Q(x) = (1/√(2π))∫ e−u²/2 du from x to ∞.
*/
