/* book Worked example 2: Q: A BPSK receiver needs Pb = 10−6. The noise density is N0 = 4.0 × 10−21 W/Hz (thermal noise at 290 K) and Rb = 1 Mb/s. Find the required Eb/N0 and the minimum received power in dBm. How much more would coherent BFSK need?
   1. Q(x) = 10−6 gives x = 4.753 (from a Q table).
   2. 2Eb/N0 = x², so Eb/N0 = 4.753²/2 = 11.30, which is 10.53 dB.
   3. Eb = 11.30 × 4.0 × 10−21 = 4.52 × 10−20 J.
   4. Received power P = Eb·Rb = 4.52 × 10−20 × 106 = 4.52 × 10−14 W.
   5. In dBm: 10 log10(4.52 × 10−14/10−3) = −103.4 dBm.
   6. Coherent BFSK needs Eb/N0 = x² = 22.59 (13.54 dB): 3.01 dB more, so −100.4 dBm.
   Answer: Eb/N0 = 10.53 dB; minimum received power −103.4 dBm (45.2 fW). Coherent BFSK needs 3 dB more, −100.4 dBm. */
